          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "A TALE OF THREE INTERSECTING LINES"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.783,
            0.06,
            0.904,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.82,
                0.152,
                0.868,
                0.162
              ],
              "content": "0774CH07"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.296,
            0.822,
            0.316
          ],
          "content": "A triangle is the most basic closed shape. As we know, it consists of:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.321,
            0.825,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "• three corner points, that we call the vertex of the triangle, and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.345,
            0.848,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "three line segments or the sides of the triangle that join the pairs of vertices."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.381,
            0.833,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "Triangles come in various shapes. Some of them are shown below."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.407,
            0.837,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.587,
            0.847,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "Observe the symbol used to denote a triangle and how the triangles are named using their vertices. While naming a triangle, the vertices can come in any order."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.642,
            0.848,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "The three sides meeting at the corners give rise to three angles that we call the angles of the triangle. For example, in ∆ABC, these angles are $\\angle CAB , \\angle ABC , \\angle BCA$ which we simply denote as $\\angle \\mathrm { A } ,   \\angle \\mathrm { B }$ and $\\angle \\complement ,$ respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.725,
            0.751,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "What happens when the three vertices lie on a straight line?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.781,
            0.497,
            0.807
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.1 Equilateral Triangles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.816,
            0.848,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": "Among all the triangles, the equilateral triangles are the most symmetric ones. These are triangles in which all the sides are of equal lengths. Let us try constructing them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.881,
            0.753,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "? Construct a triangle in which all the sides are of length 4 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
